1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:01,222
Four lines.

2
00:00:01,222 --> 00:00:06,222
That's all it takes to protect your constitutional rights during a traffic stop.

3
00:00:06,222 --> 00:00:12,282
Say the wrong thing — or say too much — and you could be handing a prosecutor the evidence they need.

4
00:00:12,282 --> 00:00:15,514
Say these four lines, and you stay in control.

5
00:00:15,514 --> 00:00:25,733
Most people lose their rights during traffic stops not because they're guilty, but because they don't know what to say — and they talk too much in a moment where every word becomes evidence.

6
00:00:25,733 --> 00:00:31,480
Here are the four lines, refined over decades of civil-rights litigation.

7
00:00:31,480 --> 00:00:35,502
Line one: I do not consent to any searches.

8
00:00:35,502 --> 00:00:40,108
Line two: Am I being detained, or am I free to go?

9
00:00:40,108 --> 00:00:44,904
Line three: I am exercising my right to remain silent.

10
00:00:44,904 --> 00:00:48,410
Line four: I would like to speak to a lawyer.

11
00:00:48,410 --> 00:00:49,388
That's it.

12
00:00:49,388 --> 00:00:50,611
Four lines.

13
00:00:50,611 --> 00:00:52,051
Memorize them.

14
00:00:52,051 --> 00:00:55,461
Practice them in the mirror until they feel boring.

15
00:00:55,461 --> 00:01:00,991
Because in the moment, you will not invent good wording — you will revert to whatever you've rehearsed.

16
00:01:00,991 --> 00:01:04,211
Now here's something that surprises most people.

17
00:01:04,211 --> 00:01:09,266
A routine traffic stop is NOT considered custody for Miranda purposes.

18
00:01:09,266 --> 00:01:15,135
That means officers can ask you questions at the roadside — 'Do you know why I pulled you over?

19
00:01:15,135 --> 00:01:16,739
Have you been drinking?

20
00:01:16,739 --> 00:01:18,260
Where are you headed?'

21
00:01:18,260 --> 00:01:20,543
— without reading you your rights.

22
00:01:20,543 --> 00:01:23,817
And your answers are still admissible in court.

23
00:01:23,817 --> 00:01:27,391
That question — 'Do you know why I pulled you over?'

24
00:01:27,391 --> 00:01:28,614
— is a trap.

25
00:01:28,614 --> 00:01:31,204
It's designed to get an admission.

26
00:01:31,204 --> 00:01:34,578
No matter how you answer, you're giving them evidence.

27
00:01:34,578 --> 00:01:40,624
The Innocence Project has documented over 375 DNA exonerations.

28
00:01:40,624 --> 00:01:47,105
Roughly 30% involved false confessions or statements from people who thought talking would clear them.

29
00:01:47,105 --> 00:01:48,600
Innocent people.

30
00:01:48,600 --> 00:01:51,331
Convicted because they said too much.

31
00:01:51,331 --> 00:01:53,165
So what does this all mean?

32
00:01:53,165 --> 00:01:56,299
It means the roadside is not where you win.

33
00:01:56,299 --> 00:01:57,391
Court is.

34
00:01:57,391 --> 00:02:04,538
Your job during a traffic stop is not to prove your innocence — it's to preserve your ability to fight later.

35
00:02:04,538 --> 00:02:07,948
Police can legally lie to you about evidence.

36
00:02:07,948 --> 00:02:11,739
They can claim your friend is in the next room confessing.

37
00:02:11,739 --> 00:02:14,646
They can say they already have fingerprints.

38
00:02:14,646 --> 00:02:16,725
None of it has to be true.

39
00:02:16,725 --> 00:02:21,277
The only defense is to say nothing that depends on what they tell you.

40
00:02:21,277 --> 00:02:26,315
And here's the part most people miss: you must verbally invoke your rights.

41
00:02:26,315 --> 00:02:32,432
The Supreme Court held in Salinas v. Texas that silence alone can be used against you.

42
00:02:32,432 --> 00:02:34,239
You have to say the words.

43
00:02:34,239 --> 00:02:35,323
Out loud.

44
00:02:35,323 --> 00:02:36,277
Clearly.

45
00:02:36,277 --> 00:02:37,445
Every time.

46
00:02:37,445 --> 00:02:38,668
Four lines.

47
00:02:38,668 --> 00:02:40,108
Memorize them.

48
00:02:40,108 --> 00:02:41,426
Practice them.

49
00:02:41,426 --> 00:02:49,714
Because the next time those red and blue lights appear in your rearview mirror, what you say in the next sixty seconds could change everything.

50
00:02:49,714 --> 00:02:51,508
This is Legal Brief.

51
00:02:51,508 --> 00:02:54,101
Subscribe for the next breakdown.

